# FormEasy

A DepEd form-filling tool. Currently supports **SF9** (Learner's Report Card) for Grade 11/12,
Academic/TechPro tracks. SF10 (Form 137) is planned — the "Select Form" screen already has a
slot for it.

## What it does

1. **Select Form** → SF9 (SF10 coming soon)
2. **Template** → pick the exact grade/track SF9 variant
3. **Subjects & Electives** → for every elective slot, choose the real DepEd cluster + subject
   (pulled from the official HELPER sheet catalog — 5 Academic clusters / 80 subjects,
   11 TechPro clusters / 49 subjects)
4. **Class Setup** → auto-filled from the uploaded template's own INPUT DATA sheet
5. **Roster** → one CSV upload (LRN, Name, DOB, Sex) — becomes the master learner list
6. **Grades, Attendance & Remarks**
   - Grades CSV: LRN, Name, then Term 1/2/3 for every configured subject
   - Attendance CSV: LRN, Name, then 11 monthly columns (Jun–Apr)
   - Remarks & Comments: set per learner, per term, via a preset dropdown (62 canned
     comments in English and Filipino — independent lists, not paired translations) or
     free text
7. **Preview & Download** → tap any learner for an instant, pixel-accurate live preview of
   their two-page report card, then download as PDF (individually if ≤5 selected, or as one
   ZIP if more)

Age is computed automatically from DOB, as of **October 31 of the school year's first year**
(e.g. school year "2026-2027" → age as of Oct 31, 2026).

## Why the report card looks exactly like the real SF9

The `data/layout/*.json` files are not hand-designed — they were extracted directly from the
real DepEd SF9 templates using `openpyxl` (column widths, row heights, per-cell fonts, borders,
alignment, merged ranges, and the exact pixel position of the embedded school/DepEd logos, all
converted from Excel's native units into browser pixels). `js/renderer.js` replays that data as
absolutely-positioned HTML, and `js/app.js` only overwrites the specific cells that hold
per-student data (name, LRN, grades, etc.) — everything else on the page is the original
template's content and styling, untouched.

## Running it locally

Browsers block `fetch()` of local JSON/image files under the `file://` protocol, so you need to
serve this over HTTP — you can't just double-click `index.html`.

```bash
# from the project folder:
python3 -m http.server 8080
# or:
npx serve .
```

Then open `http://localhost:8080`.

## Deploying

This is a fully static site (no build step, no backend) — it deploys as-is to GitHub Pages,
Netlify, Vercel, or any static host. For GitHub Pages:

```bash
git init
git add .
git commit -m "FormEasy v1.0.0"
git remote add origin <your-repo-url>
git push -u origin main
```

Then enable GitHub Pages on the repo (Settings → Pages → deploy from `main` branch, root).

## Project structure

```
index.html              Entry point
css/style.css           All styling (Montserrat self-hosted via @font-face)
js/config.js            Template definitions, cell bindings, data loaders, age calculation
js/renderer.js          Generic Excel-layout-spec → pixel-accurate HTML renderer
js/app.js               Application state, UI wiring, CSV parsing, PDF/ZIP generation
data/electives_catalog.json    Official Academic/TechPro elective clusters & subjects
data/remarks_catalog.json      62 preset remarks (English + Filipino, independent lists)
data/layout/*.json             Extracted per-template pixel/font/border/image layouts
assets/images/                 Logo images extracted from the official templates
assets/fonts/                  Montserrat static weights (400/500/600/700/800)
assets/templates/*.xlsx        The 4 source SF9 templates (read at runtime for
                                pre-filling Class Setup; not otherwise modified)
```

## Known limitations / things to verify before real use

- **Female roster row offsets** on the SF9 print layout were measured directly from the
  templates and cross-checked across all 4 variants, but please verify against a real class
  with female learners before trusting it for an actual report card run.
- **CDN dependencies**: JSZip, html2canvas, and jsPDF load from cdnjs. The app's own logic
  works fully offline once loaded, but the very first load (and any load in a fresh browser
  cache) needs internet access to fetch these three scripts.
- The **General Average** calculation is a units-weighted average of each subject's own
  average of whatever terms have data — this matches the intent of the original template's
  formulas but isn't a byte-for-byte formula replay (since the PDF is a static rendering, not
  a live spreadsheet).
- If DepEd revises SF9 templates, the JSON files under `data/layout/` will need to be
  re-extracted from the new templates using the same technique (extractor script available on
  request).

